{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>Benjamin is a ravening wolf; in the morning he eats the prey, and at evening he divides the spoil.</b> This requires examination: what is the significance of indicating evening and morning, and why is it called once 'spoil' (שָׁלָל) and once 'prey' (עַד), and why here the expression 'he eats' (יֹאכַל) is used and afterward 'he divides' (יְחַלֵּק)? And this is because it is well known that anything a person asks for in the proper manner is given to him with a radiant countenance — that is, in the morning — and anything a person asks for not in the proper manner is given to him in the evening with a darkened countenance, as Rashi explained on the verse ״בְּתֵת ה׳ לָכֶם בָּעֶרֶב בָּשָׂר לֶאֱכֹל וְלֶחֶם בַּבֹּקֶר לִשְׂבֹּעַ״ (Exodus 16:8). And anything that is necessary for a person — that is, enough for his eating to satisfy the hunger of his household — is the thing given with a radiant countenance, and his bread will not fail him forever and to eternity (עַד). But all the superfluities that a person asks for beyond what is enough for his eating — in the end he will leave his wealth to others and divide it to others, or perhaps to the one who marries his widow, while he himself is stripped and removed from those superfluities given to him in the evening. Therefore it says ״בַּבֹּקֶר״ — that is, the thing given to him with a radiant countenance; this is the thing which 'he eats' (יֹאכַל), for only enough for his eating is given to him with a radiant countenance, and it is given to him לָעַד. For even though עַד also means spoil (שָׁלָל), in any case it was mentioned in the expression ״עַד״ which alludes to eternity. ״וְלָעֶרֶב״ — what is given to him with a darkened countenance, that is the thing which 'he divides' (יְחַלֵּק) to others, while he is stripped of it, for the meaning of the expression שָׁלָל includes the notion of removal, as in ״וְנָשַׁל הַבַּרְזֶל״ (Deuteronomy 19:5) — an expression of stripping and removal." "And he juxtaposed this matter, the blessing of 'a ravening wolf,' to rebuke also the judges of Israel, that they should not tear in battle more than is necessary, as it is written (Zephaniah 3:3) ״שֹׁפְטֶיהָ זְאֵבֵי עֶרֶב לֹא גָרְמוּ לַבֹּקֶר״ — the prophet indicts them for tearing more than was necessary; therefore he called them 'wolves of the evening,' and said 'they left nothing for the morning,' for they did not seek what was necessary, which is given in the morning. And so he indicts Saul, who was of the tribe of Benjamin: ״וַתַּעַט אֶל הַשָּׁלָל״ (1 Samuel 15:19) — for he did not remember Jacob's blessing to Benjamin. But of Abraham it is written (Genesis 14:24) ״בִּלְעָדַי אֲשֶׁר אָכְלוּ הַנְּעָרִים״ etc. — he took only what was enough for their eating, and did not apportion them superfluous spoil. And with this he drew the parable from the wolf that tears for the purpose of its food, while at times it tears and destroys to no benefit — and this is a precious interpretation." "And the Ba'al ha-Turim concludes that the initial letters of the beginning of the blessings of all the tribes add up to three hundred sixty-five (שס״ה), the number of the days of the sun, and the final letters add up to three hundred fifty-four (שנ״ד), the number of the days of the moon, because in this world their entire dominion resembles the moon — sometimes it is built up, sometimes it is breached; so too at times they are diminished in kingship. But in the time when the shoot (צִיץ) shall blossom and a righteous sprout (צֶמַח צַדִּיק) shall grow, whose name is that of the messiah who preceded the world (Pesahim 54a), then it is written (Psalms 89:37) ״כִּסְאוֹ כַשֶּׁמֶשׁ נֶגְדִּי״ — that their kingship will be eternal like the sun. And therefore the sun is alluded to in the initial letters, a hint to the name of the messiah who preceded the world; and this is the secret of זֶרַח and פֶּרֶץ that the Sages expounded regarding the sun (חַמָּה) and the moon (לְבָנָה). And this is the significance of the evening and the morning that are mentioned here regarding Benjamin."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:34:53.734166522Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}